    "evidence": "For a fixed amount of gas, a process in which its pressure and volume change while it remains a gas and its temperature stays constant is called an isothermal process.",
    "supportingContext": "The last sentence identifies unchanged gas temperature as the defining condition, but it addresses the thermostat requirement; it does not explicitly detach the remains-gas condition from the preceding definition.",
    "problem": "This is a definition-scope ambiguity, not a claim that the sentence literally says only gases can undergo isothermal processes. In a paragraph introducing the term, a child can still read remaining gaseous as a condition for being isothermal. Naming thermodynamic state and phase is not enough to make clear that the phase restriction belongs only to this Boyle-law comparison. An isothermal process is defined by unchanged system temperature, not by a necessary gaseous phase or necessary simultaneous pressure and volume changes.",
    "requiredAction": "Separate the general constant-temperature process definition from the local Boyle-law assumptions. Explicitly confine remaining gaseous and the pressure-volume change to the gas sample discussed here; do not redefine all isothermal processes as necessarily gaseous or necessarily phase-preserving. Retain the source-attributed heat-reservoir paragraph, original formulas/numbers/references/snapshots and the already corrected same-temperature state comparison.",
    "acceptanceForAffectedText": "English alone states the temperature criterion for an isothermal process and treats the remains-gas condition as a Boyle-law assumption, not part of the general definition. A child can distinguish individual states, the process connecting them, and a physical phase change.",
